--- note
## Hardware
Gemma 4 is trained on **TPU v5p and TPU v6e** (Table 2). Each model configuration is tuned to
**minimize training step time**. The chip counts are large: e.g. 12B on **v5p with 12,288 chips**, 31B
on **v6e with 10,240 chips**, E4B and 26B-A4B on **v6e with 6,144 chips**, E2B on **v6e with 4,096
chips**.

--- image
learning/assets/gemma4/t02_pretrain_infra.png
Table 2 — pre-training infrastructure. Each model's parallelism is a product of three shardings: **Data**, **Sequence (Seq)**, and **Replica**. E.g. E2B = 16 data × 8 seq × 32 replica shards over 4,096 v6e chips.

--- note
## Parallelism & sharding
- The **optimizer state is sharded with an implementation of ZeRO-3** (Ren et al., 2021) — the
  optimizer moments are partitioned across chips instead of replicated, which is what makes large
  models fit.
- For **multi-pod** training they do a **data-replica reduction over the data-center network**, using
  the **Pathways** approach (Barham et al., 2022).
- The software stack is **single-controller JAX + Pathways**, with the **GSPMD partitioner** (Xu et
  al., 2021) and the **MegaScale XLA compiler**.

--- note
## Slice-Granularity Elasticity — resilience to failures
For the larger models they use **Slice-Granularity Elasticity** (Gemini Team, 2025): on a **localized
hardware failure**, training **continues with fewer "slices" of TPU chips** rather than halting. This
reconfiguration **cuts the interruption delay from many minutes to a few seconds** — a big deal at
10k-chip scale, where failures are frequent and a full restart is expensive.

--- note
## Takeaway
The infra story is "standard-but-industrial-scale JAX/TPU": ZeRO-3 optimizer sharding, GSPMD/Pathways
partitioning, XLA compilation, and elasticity for fault tolerance. The report gives chip counts and
sharding factors (Table 2) but not wall-clock training time or cost — so we do not estimate those.
